Patch 40. In the series since 2026-10-17, after patch 39.

WinSystemClock::to_sys returned a std::chrono::system_clock::time_point. libstdc++ counts
system_clock in signed 64-bit nanoseconds, which reach only the years 1677 to 2262, so a FILETIME
outside them overflowed: the NT epoch (FILETIME 0, 1601-01-01) came out as 2185-07-21. MSVC's
system_clock counts 100 ns and libc++'s microseconds, so only Linux builds were wrong. Besides
four chrono_test cases that failed on Linux, the guest saw it: RtlTimeToTimeFields and
RtlTimeFieldsToTime (xboxkrnl_rtl.cpp) go through these conversions, and a zero or very old
FILETIME, or a date after 2262, gave a wrong date. to_sys now returns a sys_time in the clock's
own 100 ns units (about 29000 years either way), from_sys takes any sys_time, and
RtlTimeFieldsToTime builds its time in 100 ns.

Test: chrono_test, with a date after 2262 (2300-03-01 12:34:56.789) besides 1601; its
decompose and recompose mirror the two RTL functions. Without the change 4 cases and 19
assertions fail on Linux (the 2300 case among them); with it all 101 assertions pass, and on the
Android tablet. Dates 1677-2262 convert as before.

